.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt USERADD 8
.Os Belka
.Sh NAME
.Nm useradd ,
.Nm usermod ,
.Nm userdel ,
.Nm groupadd ,
.Nm groupmod ,
.Nm groupdel ,
.Nm acctrecover
.Nd administer local text user and group accounts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm useradd
.Op Fl m
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar group
.Op Fl G Ar groups
.Op Fl d Ar home
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Fl c Ar comment
.Ar name
.Nm usermod
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar group
.Op Fl G Ar groups
.Op Fl d Ar home
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Fl c Ar comment
.Op Fl l Ar login
.Ar name
.Nm userdel
.Ar name
.Nm groupadd
.Op Fl g Ar gid
.Ar name
.Nm groupmod
.Op Fl g Ar gid
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Ar group
.Nm groupdel
.Ar group
.Nm acctrecover
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These commands allow the superuser to maintain the local
.Pa /etc/passwd ,
.Pa /etc/shadow ,
and
.Pa /etc/group
files.
They share the password-file lock with
.Xr passwd 1
and
.Xr chsh 1 .
.Pp
.Nm useradd
creates a password-locked account with group
.Dq users ,
home
.Pa /home/name ,
and shell
.Pa /bin/sh
by default.
Use
.Xr passwd 1
to set the initial password.
Automatic user and group identifiers are selected from unused values
in the range 1000 through 59999.
.Pp
The options have the following meanings:
.Bl -tag -width "-G groups"
.It Fl u Ar uid
Set a unique numeric user identifier.
.It Fl g Ar group
For user commands, select an existing primary group by name or number.
For group commands, set a unique numeric group identifier.
.It Fl G Ar groups
Set supplementary groups from a comma-separated list of group names or numbers.
An empty list clears supplementary membership.
.It Fl d Ar home
Set an absolute home directory path.
.It Fl s Ar shell
Set an absolute path to an executable shell.
.It Fl c Ar comment
Set the account comment.
.It Fl m
Create the home directory for a new user and assign its ownership.
An existing directory is used as supplied.
.It Fl l Ar login
Rename a user, updating shadow and supplementary membership.
.It Fl n Ar name
Rename a group.
.El
.Pp
.Nm usermod
changes the specified account fields.
Identifier and home changes update the account records.
File ownership and home contents are administered separately with
.Xr chown 1
and file utilities.
.Nm groupmod
also updates users whose primary group has the old numeric identifier.
.Nm groupdel
refuses to remove a primary group.
.Nm userdel
removes the account, shadow record, and supplementary memberships.
Home contents can be removed separately with
.Xr rm 1 .
.Pp
Names begin with a letter or underscore and contain letters, digits,
underscores, dots, or hyphens.
Names must be shorter than LOGIN_NAME_MAX.
Numeric identifiers range from zero through INT_MAX, matching the
current libc account parser.
Passwd records must fit within the libc record buffer.
The supplementary group limit is obtained through sysconf.
.Sh RECOVERY
Updates stage all three files in the root-only
.Pa /etc/.accttxn
directory.
A ready marker records complete, synced backups and replacements before
publication.
A committed marker records successful publication of all three files.
.Pp
.Nm acctrecover
rolls back an interrupted update or removes a completed transaction's backups.
It runs at boot before services, and every account-file writer performs
recovery when acquiring the shared lock.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chsh 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1
